   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the transportation law, in relation to information concerning\nservices for human trafficking victims in commercial service airports",
    "basePrintNo": "S8710",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8710-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 34,
       "fullName": "Alessandra Biaggi",
       "imgName": "1051_Alessandra_Biaggi.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1231,
       "sessionMemberId": 1799,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "BIAGGI"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §14-n, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8710\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to information\nconcerning services for human trafficking victims in commercial service\nairports\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require airports to provide information concerning services for human\ntrafficking victims in public restrooms.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new section 14-n to the transportation law to require\ncommercial service airports and general aviation airports to make infor-\nmational cards or signs available in public restrooms, which contain\ninformation on services for trafficking victims, including the national\nhuman trafficking hotline number.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHuman trafficking is a global phenomenon that generates $150 billion in\nannual profits. In 2021, an estimated 25 million people were trafficked.\nThe convenience and anonymity of airports makes it convenient for traf-\nfickers to move victims.\n\nBecause airports are known hubs used to traffic victims both interna-\ntionally and domestically, they are an ideal location to provide infor-\nmation about victim services. This information can not only inform\nvictims, but also other travelers about the availability of services.\n\nMany victims are heavily supervised and controlled while in transit,\npreventing them from safely seeking help or finding resources without\nfear of retaliation or threat. This bill will require operators of\ncommercial services airports and general aviation airports to post\ninformational signs or cards in all public restrooms. The signs and\ncards must include information on human trafficking services and must\nprominently display the national human trafficking hotline number.\n\nThrough this legislation, airports can assume a more prominent role in\nresponding to and preventing human trafficking. It will also afford\nvictims the tools and resources to hopefully escape their captors.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the sixtieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
